<section class="section section--tight" aria-labelledby="life-title">
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="section-head">
      <h2 id="life-title">The life of one credit deal</h2>
      <p>Each step leaves a record. Where businesses go wrong is usually a step recorded late, or not at all. This is an illustration.</p>
    </header>
    <ol class="cycle">
      <li><h3>You buy on credit</h3><p>Supplier bill, transport, and goods checked in. Your payable and stock both rise.</p></li>
      <li><h3>The bill reaches GST data</h3><p>Check it in your supplier view and GSTR-2B before you rely on the credit.</p></li>
      <li><h3>You sell on credit</h3><p>Tax invoice and, where applicable, an e-way bill. Your receivable rises and stock falls.</p></li>
      <li><h3>The customer pays part</h3><p>Match the payment to specific bills, not just to the account.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Goods come back, or a discount is given</h3><p>Issue the right credit note. An informal adjustment breaks the ledger and the return.</p></li>
      <li><h3>You pay the supplier</h3><p>On time, partly because of the credit rule explained below.</p></li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--tint" aria-labelledby="rule-title">
  <div class="wrap two-col">
    <div>
      <h2 id="rule-title">The 180-day rule traders should know</h2>
      <p>Under section 16(2) of the CGST Act and rule 37, as explained by commentators, if you do not pay a supplier the value and tax within 180 days of the invoice date, the related input tax credit must be reversed, with interest. It can generally be claimed again once you pay.</p>
      <p class="para">So long credit from your supplier has a cost beyond the bill. Ask your accountant for a monthly list of unpaid bills approaching 180 days. Sources differ on some details, such as part payments and interest, so get advice on specific cases.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h2>Reports worth asking for each month</h2>
      <ul class="tick-list plain-list">
        <li><strong>Receivables by age:</strong> 0 to 30, 31 to 60, 61 to 90 and over 90 days</li>
        <li><strong>Payables by age,</strong> with the 180-day items flagged</li>
        <li><strong>Party ledger confirmations</strong> for large balances</li>
        <li><strong>Stock by item,</strong> or at least by category, with slow movers</li>
        <li><strong>Gross margin</strong> by month, and by product line if you can</li>
        <li><strong>GST credit in your books compared with GSTR-2B</strong></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" aria-labelledby="problems-title">
  <div class="wrap layout-side">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2 id="problems-title" class="first-h2">Where traders' books usually go wrong</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>On-account receipts.</strong> Money is recorded against the party but not against bills, so nobody knows which bill is unpaid.</li>
        <li><strong>Balances never confirmed.</strong> Your ledger and the customer's differ, and the gap appears when it is too late to resolve.</li>
        <li><strong>Stock known only from memory.</strong> Without records, profit is a guess. Discuss how often to count and how to value it.</li>
        <li><strong>Cash discounts and "kachha" adjustments.</strong> Unrecorded deals make the books and GST returns unreliable, and can create serious tax exposure.</li>
        <li><strong>Same ledger under several names.</strong> Duplicate party names split balances. Clean your party list.</li>
        <li><strong>Cash limits ignored.</strong> Income tax rules restrict certain cash transactions, so ask your adviser what applies.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="capital">Working capital in one sentence</h2>
      <p>Work out how many days of sales are stuck in stock plus customer dues, less the days of credit your suppliers give you. If this number grows while sales stay flat, you are funding other people's businesses. Good monthly reports let you see it early.</p>

      <h2 id="choose">What to ask an accountant who says they handle traders</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>How do you match receipts to bills? Show me a sample ledger.</li>
        <li>How will you track bills nearing 180 days?</li>
        <li>How do you handle returns, rate differences and transport charges?</li>
        <li>How often will you reconcile with GSTR-2B?</li>
        <li>Are you comfortable with my invoice volume in the busy season?</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="fit">Trader or retailer?</h2>
      <p>If you sell mostly to walk-in consumers, see {% routeLink "/industries/accounting-for-retail-shops/", "accounting for retail shops" %}, where daily cash and UPI matter more than party credit. For market context, see {% routeLink "/locations/wholesale-trading/", "Delhi's wholesale and trading markets" %}.</p>

